At steady state, \[\text{Inductor} \rightarrow \text{Short circuit},
\qquad
\text{Capacitor} \rightarrow \text{Open circuit}\] <img src="img/0f3573d163777107.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> The \(10~\text{k}\Omega\) and \(40~\text{k}\Omega\) resistors form a voltage divider across the \(100~\text{V}\) source. Hence, \[V_{C1}
=
100\times\frac{40}{10+40}
=
80~\text{V}\] Then using capacitive voltage division, \[V_{C2}
=
80\times\frac{3}{2+3}
=
48~\text{V}\] \[V_{C3}
=
80\times\frac{2}{2+3}
=
32~\text{V}\] Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{B. }80~\text{V},~48~\text{V},~32~\text{V}}\]

The small-signal equivalent circuit is shown below. <img src="img/469895f306666ec7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,237px)"> Combining the capacitors connected between the base and collector, \[C_x=C_C+C_\mu=25+0.5=25.5\,\text{pF}\] The voltage gain is \[A_v=-g_mR_C
=-(5\times10^{-3})(10\times10^3)
=-50\] Applying Miller's theorem, the equivalent circuit becomes \[\SolutionFigureNoNumber[width=0.5\linewidth]{img/ch4_bjts_solutions/4_96_1b.png}\] The equivalent input capacitance is \[C_{Mi}=C_x(1-A_v)
=25.5(1+50)
=1300.5\,\text{pF}\] \[C_i=C_\pi+C_{Mi}
=1.5+1300.5
=1302\,\text{pF}\] The resistance seen by the input capacitor is \[R_i=R_S\parallel r_\pi
=10\,\text{k}\Omega\parallel200\,\text{k}\Omega
=9.52\,\text{k}\Omega\] Hence, the input pole is \[f_{Hi}
=\frac{1}{2\pi R_iC_i}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(9.52\times10^3)(1302\times10^{-12})}
=12.85\times10^3\,\text{Hz}\] The equivalent output capacitance is \[C_{Mo}
=C_x\left(1-\frac{1}{A_v}\right)
=25.5\left(1+\frac{1}{50}\right)
=26.01\,\text{pF}\] Therefore, \[f_{Ho}
=\frac{1}{2\pi R_CC_{Mo}}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(10\times10^3)(26.01\times10^{-12})}
=611.9\times10^3\,\text{Hz}\] Since \(f_{Hi}&lt;f_{Ho},\) the input pole is dominant. Therefore, \[\boxed{f_H=12.85\times10^3\,\text{Hz}}\]

The overall transconductance of the Darlington pair is, \[g_m=\frac{I_{c2}}{V_{be}}\] From the figure, <img src="img/28cd47ca91e11585.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,128px)"> \[V_{be}=V_{be1}+V_{be2}\] Since \[I_{c1}=g_{m1}V_{be1},
\qquad
I_{c2}=g_{m2}V_{be2},\] \[V_{be1}=\frac{I_{c1}}{g_{m1}},
\qquad
V_{be2}=\frac{I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}\] Therefore, \[V_{be}
=\frac{I_{c1}}{g_{m1}}
+\frac{I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}\] From the Darlington connection, \[I_{E1}=I_{B2},
\qquad
I_{c1}\approx I_{E1},
\qquad
I_{c2}=\beta I_{B2}\] Hence, \[I_{c2}\approx\beta I_{c1}\] Since, \(g_m=\dfrac{I_C}{V_T},\) \[g_{m2}
=\frac{I_{c2}}{V_T}
=\frac{\beta I_{c1}}{V_T}
=\beta g_{m1}\] Therefore, \[\frac{I_{c1}}{g_{m1}}
=
\frac{I_{c2}/\beta}{g_{m2}/\beta}
=
\frac{I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}\] Substituting in the expression for \(V_{be}\), \[V_{be}
=\frac{I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}
+\frac{I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}
=\frac{2I_{c2}}{g_{m2}}\] Hence, \[g_m
=\frac{I_{c2}}{V_{be}}
=\frac{I_{c2}}{2I_{c2}/g_{m2}}
=\frac{g_{m2}}{2}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;0.5\,g_{m2}}\]

Since the amplifier has a very large input impedance, the input current flows only through the feedback impedance \(Z\). Therefore, \[I_i=\frac{V_i-V_o}{Z}\] Given the voltage gain of the amplifier, \[V_o=KV_i\] Substituting, \[I_i=\frac{V_i-KV_i}{Z}
=\frac{V_i(1-K)}{Z}\] Hence, the input impedance is \[Z_{in}
=\frac{V_i}{I_i}
=\frac{V_i}{V_i(1-K)/Z}
=\frac{Z}{1-K}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;\frac{Z}{1-K}}\]
